Revision history for Getopt-Long-Bash

2.1.0 2026-11-26T09:29:55Z

    - INCOMPATIBLE CHANGES in EXIT_ON_ERROR.  Scripts which disable
      EXIT_ON_ERROR to leave undefined options in the arguments, such
      as the options of a subcommand, have to be updated: disable
      PERMUTE instead, and give the global options before the
      subcommand
    - INCOMPATIBLE: with EXIT_ON_ERROR disabled, getoptlong parse
      stops at the first error and returns 1, and leaves the
      arguments as they are.  An undefined option was left in the
      arguments, and it went wrong in a cluster of short options:
      "-w170" for an undefined -w gave the argument before it three
      times
    - INCOMPATIBLE: a long option without its argument and a
      validation error also return 1 with EXIT_ON_ERROR disabled.
      They exited the script regardless of EXIT_ON_ERROR
    - With EXIT_ON_ERROR disabled, SILENT suppresses all the error
      messages of parsing, not only those of getopts
    - Nothing changes with EXIT_ON_ERROR enabled, the default
    - New option type "*": the arguments up to "--" are collected into
      an array as they are, as "--names a b --"

2.0.0 2026-11-23T11:38:05Z

    - INCOMPATIBLE CHANGES in getoptlong callback.  Scripts which
      register a callback with fixed arguments, or two or more
      callbacks in one call, have to be updated.  The former gets an
      error when the callback is registered:
    - INCOMPATIBLE: fixed arguments of a callback are passed before
      the option name and value, so that a common function can be
      called from several options and behave differently by the
      arguments: "getoptlong callback quiet set_level 0" calls
      "set_level 0 quiet 1".  They were passed between the option
      name and the value
    - INCOMPATIBLE: getoptlong callback registers one callback at a
      time, and takes the arguments after the function as fixed
      arguments, as the document says.  They were taken as more pairs
      of an option and a function.  A fixed argument or a function
      name with white space, such as "func arg" of 1.5.0, is an error
    - getoptlong callback gives an error for an undefined option, and
      takes an alias name as the option it stands for.  They were
      silently ignored

1.5.0 2026-11-23T01:29:03Z

    - Security fix: the value given to an option with a regex
      validation (=(...)) was evaluated by the shell, so a command
      substitution in the value was executed, and such a value could
      pass the validation.  All the versions up to 1.4.0 are
      affected.  =i and =f are not (#5)
    - The pattern of a regex validation is not evaluated by the shell
      either: quotes and $ in the pattern are regex characters
    - The return value of a callback function is ignored, as
      documented.  A callback returning non-zero made the script die
      with "callback function ... is not defined"
    - A callback registered with two or more additional arguments was
      silently not registered
    - getoptlong command shows the library version with --version
    - getoptlong with no subcommand gives a proper error under set -u,
      and the default usage line works with a script path containing
      spaces

1.4.0 2026-11-22T16:12:28Z

    - Backslashes in the value of a list (@) or hash (%) option are
      kept as is.  They were taken as escapes and removed (#4)

1.3.0 2026-11-17T01:41:41Z

    - An option which takes an optional argument (?) can have an
      initial value, which was an error.  It is the default: the
      variable has it when the option is not specified, and --no-
      unsets the variable

1.2.0 2026-11-16T09:05:11Z

    - --no- with an option which takes an optional argument (?) unsets
      the variable, as when the option is not specified.  It gave the
      empty string, the same as the option without a value.  A value
      assigned to the variable before the options are parsed works as
      a default, which --no- removes.  A callback of such an option
      has to use ${var-} under set -u

1.1.1 2026-11-10T10:47:07Z

    - Pass-through keeps a value as one element: it was split at
      spaces and expanded as a file name pattern
    - Pass-through passes the value of an optional argument, in the
      form of "--option=value": it was dropped

1.1.0 2026-10-27T09:18:40Z

    - Auto-initialize only when getoptlong.sh is sourced as a file, so
      that the code embedded in a script does not take the script's
      first argument for an options array, and document how to embed it
    - Source the library with "-" in the multi-step examples

1.0.0 2026-08-27T14:22:49Z

    - Require bash 4.4 or later, and report the required version when
      running on an older bash

0.8.1 2026-03-30T17:04:10Z

    - Document negative alias feature and getoptlong version subcommand

0.8.0 2026-03-09T07:53:38Z

    - Add negative alias (~) for option negation (short and long)
    - Show deny aliases with ~/~~ prefix in help output

0.7.2 2026-02-11T09:40:06Z

    - Fix numeric comparison bug in permute loop (string > vs -gt)
    - Add permute mode tests

0.7.1 2026-01-17T02:19:55Z

    - Fix callback function naming for hyphenated options (list-themes calls list_themes)

0.7.0 2025-12-30T12:52:21Z

    - Fix passthru negation with short option aliases (--no-v was becoming -v)

0.6.0 2025-12-24T07:22:23Z

    - Allow whitespace around mark character keys for alignment
    - Improve documentation with clearer examples
    - Update Tutorial with spaced option definition format

0.5.0 2025-12-23T12:10:40Z

    - Initial CPAN release
    - Bash library for Getopt::Long-like option parsing
